## Proof

Let $G$ be a connected simple graph with $\alpha(G) = |P|$, where $P$ is the set of pendant vertices (vertices of degree 1).

### Step 1: The condition forces $|V(G)| \geq 3$

If $|V| = 1$: the vertex has degree 0, so $|P|=0$ but $\alpha=1$. Condition fails.
If $|V| = 2$ and $G = K_2$: both vertices are pendant, $|P|=2$ but $\alpha=1$. Condition fails.
So the condition implies $|V| \geq 3$.

### Step 2: $P$ is a maximum (hence maximal) independent set

For $|V| \geq 3$, no two pendant vertices are adjacent: if pendant vertices $u, v$ were adjacent, then $u$'s only neighbor is $v$ and $v$'s only neighbor is $u$, so the connected component is $K_2$, forcing $G = K_2$ and $|V|=2$, contradiction.

So $P$ is independent. Since $|P| = \alpha(G)$, it is a **maximum** independent set, hence **maximal**: every vertex $v \notin P$ is adjacent to some vertex in $P$.

### Step 3: Every non-pendant vertex is a support vertex; $V = P \cup S$

Let $S$ be the set of **support vertices** (vertices adjacent to at least one pendant vertex). By maximality of $P$, every $v \notin P$ is adjacent to some pendant vertex, so $v \in S$. Thus $V = P \cup S$ (disjoint union).

### Step 4: $G[S]$ is connected

Since $G$ is connected and pendant vertices are leaves (degree 1, cannot be internal vertices of any path), any path between two support vertices lies entirely in $S$. So $G[S]$ is connected.

### Step 5: Every total dominating set contains $S$

Let $T$ be a total dominating set. For any pendant vertex $p$ with support $s(p)$, $p$ must have a neighbor in $T$. Since $p$'s only neighbor is $s(p)$, we need $s(p) \in T$. As every support vertex is the support of at least one pendant vertex, $S \subseteq T$.

### Step 6: Characterization of total dominating sets

A set $T \supseteq S$ is a total dominating set iff every $s \in S$ has a neighbor in $T$ (pendant vertices are automatically dominated by their supports in $S$).

Let $I = \{s \in S : s \text{ has no neighbor in } S\}$ be the set of isolated vertices in $G[S]$. For $s \in I$, $s$ needs a pendant neighbor in $T$. For $s \notin I$, $s$ already has a neighbor in $S \subseteq T$.

### Step 7: Structure of $I$

Since $G[S]$ is connected:
- **If $|S| = 1$:** $G[S]$ is a single isolated vertex, so $I = S = \{s\}$. The graph is a star $K_{1,|P|}$.
- **If $|S| \geq 2$:** $G[S]$ is connected with $\geq 2$ vertices, so no vertex is isolated: $I = \emptyset$.

### Step 8: All minimal total dominating sets have the same cardinality

**Case 1: $|S| \geq 2$ ($I = \emptyset$).** Then $S$ itself is a total dominating set: every pendant vertex has its support in $S$, and every support vertex has a neighbor in $S$ (since $G[S]$ is connected with $|S|\geq 2$). $S$ is minimal (removing any $s \in S$ leaves its pendant neighbors undominated). Any TDS $T \supsetneq S$ contains extra pendant vertices, each of which is removable (since $I=\emptyset$, no support vertex needs a pendant neighbor), so $T$ is not minimal. **$S$ is the unique minimal TDS**, of size $|S|$.

**Case 2: $|S| = 1$ ($I = \{s\}$, star graph).** A TDS must contain $s$ (to dominate pendant vertices) and at least one pendant vertex $p$ (to dominate $s$, since $s$ has no neighbor in $S$). The set $\{s, p\}$ is a TDS: $p$ is dominated by $s$, $s$ is dominated by $p$, and all other pendant vertices are dominated by $s$. It is minimal: removing $s$ leaves pendant vertices undominated; removing $p$ leaves $s$ with no neighbor in $T$. Any TDS with more than one pendant vertex is not minimal (extra pendant vertices are removable). **The minimal TDS are exactly $\{s, p\}$ for each pendant $p$**, all of size $2$.

### Conclusion

In Case 1, all minimal total dominating sets have cardinality $|S|$. In Case 2, all minimal total dominating sets have cardinality $2$. In both cases, every minimal total dominating set is a minimum total dominating set, so $G$ is **well totally dominated**. $\blacksquare$

